that's okay. 
GitHub may show “Settings → Deploy keys” only if you have admin/owner permission on that repository.

Since you said it's your private GitHub account, let's use a simpler method: GitHub Personal Access Token (PAT) + Jenkins. This works even when Deploy Keys isn't available.

Step 1 — Create GitHub token

GitHub:

Profile picture → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Tokens (classic) → Generate new token

Select:

repo

Then click Generate token.

Copy the token immediately. It starts something like:

ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
Step 2 — Add token to Jenkins

Go to:

Jenkins → Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global credentials → Add Credentials

Select:

Kind: Username with password

Username: YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME

Password: YOUR_GITHUB_TOKEN

ID: github-private

Click Create.

Step 3 — Use your private repository

Use your normal HTTPS repository URL:

https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_PRIVATE_REPO.git

Jenkins Pipeline:

pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                git(
                    branch: 'main',
                    credentialsId: 'github-private',
                    url: 'https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_PRIVATE_REPO.git'
                )
            }
        }

        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'ls -la'
            }
        }
    }
}

#build
